    "en":  {
               "headline":  "Strong Q3 can\u0027t stop a 33% slide as the AI-wide selloff hits PLTR",
               "technical":  "The trend has turned down. On 2026-11-20 PLTR closed at 143.49 USD, down 6.63%. That puts it below all three exponential moving averages (EMAs, smoothed average prices): EMA50 175.17, EMA100 170.27 and EMA200 161.71. The EMAs are still stacked in bullish order (50 above 100 above 200), but losing the 200-day line, the usual long-term trend marker, is a serious break. Momentum is negative. MACD (the gap between a fast and a slow average) is at -9.64, below its signal line at -7.15, and the histogram is -2.49. RSI 14, a 0–100 momentum gauge where under 30 counts as oversold, is 26.38. Price sits just above the lower Bollinger Band at 140.01 (the bands mark a normal range around the 20-day average of 171.82), which shows stretched selling. The stock is 32.6% below its 52-week high of 212.94 and about 35% of the way up from the 52-week low of 106.37. Volume was 36.6M, roughly 1.46x the 20-day average, and the close came near the day\u0027s low of 142.28. Heavy volume like that confirms the selling pressure. Oversold readings can bring short bounces, but they don\u0027t confirm a bottom. Watch 140 (lower band) as nearby support and 161.71 (EMA200) as the first resistance.",
               "fundamental":  "The business is accelerating, not cracking. Q3 2026 (reported 2026-11-02): revenue was 2.31B USD (+96% y/y), against consensus near 2.18B. US commercial revenue reached 984M (+148%) and passed US government (953M) for the first time. The adjusted operating margin was 62% and net dollar retention 165%, meaning existing customers spent much more than a year earlier. That directly supports the switching-cost moat. FY2026 guidance rose to 8.53B revenue and 4.8–5.0B adjusted free cash flow. The soft spots: total contract value (TCV) grew only 6% y/y and remaining performance obligations (RPO, signed but not yet booked revenue) were flat quarter on quarter at 4.9B. Valuation is still extreme, at about 51x trailing sales (Trefis, 2026-11-06).",
               "news":  [
                            "2026-11-02: Q3 beat on revenue and EPS (0.44 USD adjusted), and FY2026 guidance was raised again. The stock still fell about 5% on 2026-11-03 as investors focused on valuation.",
                            "2026-11-03: Citi (265), Morgan Stanley (233), Piper Sandler (235) and RBC (130, Underperform) all raised price targets but kept their ratings. Phillip Securities raised its target to 248 on 2026-11-16.",
                            "2026-11-05: PLTR fell 9.7% with no company news, in an AI-wide selloff where the Nasdaq lost 5.5%. Before that, the stock had fallen for seven straight sessions.",
                            "Mid-November: reports of a leadership crisis at Nvidia, including CEO Jensen Huang\u0027s resignation, deepened AI-sector fear. Palantir has almost no China exposure and doesn\u0027t finance customer purchases, so this hit sentiment rather than its business.",
                            "2026-11-20: the DOJ charged a Russian national with spying on a Silicon Valley firm that sources told Reuters was Palantir. CNBC also reported that Trump-linked accounts bought PLTR before federal awards, though it found no sign Trump directed the trades. These are headline risks, not business changes."
                        ],
               "caution":  "Even after a 33% drop, PLTR is priced for near-perfection, at roughly 99x free cash flow and about 51x sales. If AI-sector sentiment keeps falling, high-multiple names usually fall furthest, and this one fell more than peers on 2026-11-05. Slowing bookings (TCV +6%, RPO flat quarter on quarter) could worry growth investors if they continue into Q4. Government-related headlines (the August 2026 sole-source memo scrutiny, the Trump-trade report) add political risk. The technical picture is plainly bearish until price gets back above the EMA200 at 161.71.",
               "opportunity":  "This fall looks driven by sentiment more than fundamentals. Revenue nearly doubled, guidance rose for a third time in 2026, and 165% net dollar retention shows customers expanding deeper into Foundry and AIP. RSI 26.38 and a price near the lower Bollinger Band mean the stock is statistically stretched, a state that has often come before relief rallies. Most covering analysts raised targets after Q3. For long-term holders, the moat (embedded deployments and data-integration lock-in) looks stronger, not weaker, after this quarter.",
               "risks":  [
                             "Valuation compression: at about 51x sales, PLTR could keep falling even if growth stays strong",
                             "Bookings slowdown: TCV +6% y/y and flat RPO may point to slower growth in 2027",
                             "AI-sector contagion from Nvidia leadership turmoil and the wider AI capex selloff",
                             "Political and governance headlines: sole-source contract scrutiny, the Trump-linked trading report, insider selling under 10b5-1 plans",
                             "Technical breakdown below EMA200 with high volume; a close below the 140 lower band would open more downside"
                         ],
               "summary":  "PLTR is in a sharp technical downtrend. It closed at 143.49 USD on 2026-11-20, below all three EMAs, with negative MACD, RSI oversold at 26.38 and selling on 1.46x normal volume. The fall from 212.94 comes from AI-sector fear and valuation worries, not weak results. Q3 revenue rose 96%, US commercial revenue passed government, and net dollar retention of 165% supports the switching-cost moat. The thesis is intact, but the short-term trend is bearish. Watch support near 140 and resistance at 161.71."
